00:09:37.010 --> 00:09:38.820
So on the screen, once
I turn the light on,

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:40.730
we'll be able to see cells.

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:43.360
And so I have a few
different types of cells

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:44.500
for you guys to look at.

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:49.500
So this first one is a
colon cancer cell line.

00:09:49.900 --> 00:09:51.760
So you can see that this is
what the cells look like.

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:52.810
They're growing in a dish.

00:09:52.810 --> 00:09:55.340
You have this red liquid on top,

00:09:55.340 --> 00:09:57.350
which is just their media.

00:09:57.350 --> 00:09:59.280
Basically, it has all
the nutrients they need.

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:00.410
But the cells are really small,

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:02.810
so in order to see them,
we need the microscope.

00:10:04.030 --> 00:10:05.710
So we're gonna turn the light on

00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:09.050
and then we have to focus on them.

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:21.702
So you can see these are our cells.

00:10:21.702 --> 00:10:25.390
So basically all of these little shapes,

00:10:25.390 --> 00:10:28.250
they're kind of like a weird triangle

00:10:28.250 --> 00:10:32.834
or like an elongated diamond.

00:10:32.834 --> 00:10:35.940
And so we have all the different
cells that are growing.

00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:36.920
And then we have the cell,

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:39.420
like places where there are no cells.

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:42.720
And so this is a colon cancer cell line.

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:46.330
So it grows attached to the plate.

00:10:46.330 --> 00:10:47.180
So it's adherent.

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:50.950
So you can see as I move
the plate, the cells move.

00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:53.020
And there's a few cells
that are kind of floating.

00:10:53.020 --> 00:10:54.403
Those are dead cells.

00:10:55.492 --> 00:10:57.050
On the other hand,

00:10:57.050 --> 00:11:01.510
I also have this other
cell line right here,

00:11:01.510 --> 00:11:04.960
which is a suspension cell line.

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:07.410
So it grows in suspension.

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:08.500
It doesn't adhere.

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:10.350
It doesn't attach to
the bottom of the dish.

00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:12.490
And so you can see when I move

00:11:12.490 --> 00:11:14.730
that basically the cells
move back and forth.

00:11:14.730 --> 00:11:18.815
And also, there's a few different levels

00:11:18.815 --> 00:11:20.190
at which I can focus.

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:23.320
And so these are a cell line

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:25.608
that were made from red blood cells,

00:11:25.608 --> 00:11:28.178
basically the precursors
to red blood cells.

00:11:28.178 --> 00:11:31.861
And so what they did
was they added in a gene

00:11:31.861 --> 00:11:34.700
that allowed the cells to grow forever

00:11:34.700 --> 00:11:36.320
'cause normally, your cells only grow

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:38.000
for a certain amount of time,

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:39.610
and then they stop.

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:41.640
And by adding this gene,

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:43.100
we can grow these cells forever

00:11:43.100 --> 00:11:44.610
so we can use them in the lab

00:11:44.610 --> 00:11:48.500
in order to study red
blood cell differentiation.

00:11:48.500 --> 00:11:50.890
We can see how if you take something away

00:11:50.890 --> 00:11:52.840
or you add something different,

00:11:52.840 --> 00:11:54.240
how that affects differentiation.

00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:55.890
But you can see that these cells,

00:11:56.876 --> 00:11:59.910
like I said, are suspension,

00:11:59.910 --> 00:12:02.320
whereas the other ones were more attached.

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:03.380
So for both of these,

00:12:03.380 --> 00:12:06.480
I showed you the image in the right field.

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:09.490
So this is basically just
normal transmitted light.

00:12:09.490 --> 00:12:11.830
Something else that we can do to our cells

00:12:11.830 --> 00:12:15.799
is we can add in fluorescent tags

00:12:15.799 --> 00:12:20.566
so that then our cells
have a color to them

00:12:20.566 --> 00:12:23.210
when you shine the right
laser light at them.

00:12:23.210 --> 00:12:25.140
So that's what these are.

00:12:25.140 --> 00:12:27.040
So I'll turn the light off real quick

00:12:29.256 --> 00:12:34.256
and switch over to the GFP channel.

00:12:36.570 --> 00:12:39.000
So you can see here

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:41.380
that you basically have these cells

00:12:41.380 --> 00:12:44.240
that have green in the circle like this.

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:47.360
So these are cells that have a GFP

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:52.360
or green fluorescent protein
attached to their mitochondria.

00:12:52.930 --> 00:12:55.550
So basically, all of their mitochondria,

00:12:55.550 --> 00:12:57.250
which produce the energy in the cells,

00:12:57.250 --> 00:12:58.357
are labeled with GFP.

00:12:58.357 --> 00:13:01.910
And so you can use these
to study the mitochondria.

00:13:01.910 --> 00:13:04.940
For example, if you treat
the cells with a chemical

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:08.940
and it causes an increase or
decrease in this green color,

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:11.483
or that changes the patterning of them.

00:13:13.910 --> 00:13:14.743
So yeah,

00:13:14.743 --> 00:13:17.532
so that's how fluorescence can be used

00:13:17.532 --> 00:13:21.120
in microscopy in cell culture.

