It looks like you guys were split

00:16:46.260 --> 00:16:48.100
between whether they come from cancer,

00:16:48.100 --> 00:16:50.420
normal tissue, or how they
normally grow in the body,

00:16:50.420 --> 00:16:53.520
which actually are really good guesses

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:57.190
'cause basically I showed
you a colon cancer cell line

00:16:57.190 --> 00:16:59.090
that grew adherently

00:16:59.090 --> 00:17:01.260
and I showed you a red blood cell line

00:17:01.260 --> 00:17:03.100
that grew in suspension.

00:17:03.100 --> 00:17:05.670
And so I could see how normal

00:17:05.670 --> 00:17:08.050
or cancer would be a good guess,

00:17:08.050 --> 00:17:09.220
but the correct answer would be

00:17:09.220 --> 00:17:11.690
how they normally grow in the human body.

00:17:11.690 --> 00:17:16.690
So if I had a blood cancer
cell line, for example,

00:17:17.500 --> 00:17:19.300
that would also grow in suspension.

00:17:19.300 --> 00:17:22.230
So blood, it's suspended.

00:17:22.230 --> 00:17:24.230
It's liquid inside of you

00:17:24.230 --> 00:17:26.530
and the cells are kind
of just floating around,

00:17:26.530 --> 00:17:30.210
as opposed to your colon,
your large intestine,

00:17:30.210 --> 00:17:34.943
where the cells are growing attached.

00:17:38.970 --> 00:17:40.600
Like you know, they're a sheet.

00:17:40.600 --> 00:17:45.230
So the same thing if you had
like a skin cancer cell line.

00:17:45.230 --> 00:17:47.520
That would grow adherently.

00:17:47.520 --> 00:17:50.660
And so most cell lines grow adherently.

00:17:50.660 --> 00:17:53.310
It's more the blood ones that
tend to grow in suspension.

00:17:53.310 --> 00:17:57.220
But you just have to figure
out the best way to culture.

00:17:57.220 --> 00:17:59.270
Every cell line's a little bit different.

00:18:06.260 --> 00:18:10.280
Yeah, so does anybody have any questions

00:18:10.280 --> 00:18:13.480
about anything I've shown
you with cell culture so far

00:18:13.480 --> 00:18:16.963
or anything at all before I move on?

00:18:22.250 --> 00:18:25.000
Okay.

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:26.970
So now, I'm gonna tell you guys.

00:18:26.970 --> 00:18:28.240
- [Teacher] I'm sorry.

00:18:28.240 --> 00:18:30.500
I just want to reinforce that, you know,

00:18:30.500 --> 00:18:32.810
like you should definitely ask questions.

00:18:32.810 --> 00:18:36.410
This is going to be as
interactive as you make it.

00:18:36.410 --> 00:18:39.126
So again, definitely ask
your wondering questions

00:18:39.126 --> 00:18:40.293
that you have.

00:18:41.270 --> 00:18:42.177
- [Odaelys] Yeah,

00:18:42.177 --> 00:18:44.480
you have a scientist at
your hands right now,

00:18:44.480 --> 00:18:46.510
so take advantage of it.

00:18:46.510 --> 00:18:48.730
- Yeah, and there are no stupid questions.

00:18:48.730 --> 00:18:52.820
Like please, ask me anything
I guess science related,

00:18:52.820 --> 00:18:53.653
but you know,

00:18:53.653 --> 00:18:54.486
like I love to help.

00:18:54.486 --> 00:18:59.230
So it looks like we have Mary Ellis.

00:18:59.230 --> 00:19:00.500
Is that how you pronounce your name?

00:19:00.500 --> 00:19:01.487
- [Mary] Yeah.

00:19:01.487 --> 00:19:02.633
- So what's your question?

00:19:02.633 --> 00:19:06.764
- [Mary] Okay, so isn't
cancer like when the cells

00:19:06.764 --> 00:19:08.680
like have a miscommunication

00:19:08.680 --> 00:19:10.513
and there's an error that happened?

00:19:13.150 --> 00:19:13.983
- Yeah.

00:19:13.983 --> 00:19:17.510
So it's often caused by
mutations in their DNA.

00:19:17.510 --> 00:19:21.020
So when you have your DNA
that's being replicated

00:19:21.020 --> 00:19:23.410
and you have your base pairs.

00:19:23.410 --> 00:19:25.240
Instead of like putting in A, for example,

00:19:25.240 --> 00:19:27.460
you end up with a G or something.

00:19:27.460 --> 00:19:32.450
And that causes the gene to
now either lose its function

00:19:32.450 --> 00:19:34.190
or gain extra functions.

00:19:34.190 --> 00:19:37.690
And so this happens a few
different places in the DNA.

00:19:37.690 --> 00:19:42.010
And together, they allow
the cells to proliferate,

00:19:42.010 --> 00:19:44.420
to grow uncontrollably.

00:19:44.420 --> 00:19:46.470
So they basically,

00:19:46.470 --> 00:19:47.870
normally in your body,

00:19:47.870 --> 00:19:50.690
there's a lot of regulation
of like, you know,

00:19:50.690 --> 00:19:54.050
cells can grow and then they have to stop.

00:19:54.050 --> 00:19:56.470
For example, like say you have a wound,

00:19:56.470 --> 00:19:58.660
you need to regrow a bunch of skin cells

00:19:58.660 --> 00:20:00.100
in order to fill in the wound.

00:20:00.100 --> 00:20:01.990
But once you fill in the wound,

00:20:01.990 --> 00:20:04.199
the skin cells stop growing.

00:20:04.199 --> 00:20:07.560
But with cancer, there is no stop signal.

00:20:07.560 --> 00:20:09.150
They basically have stopped listening

00:20:09.150 --> 00:20:10.000
to the rest of the body.

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:13.160
So they just keep growing,
keep growing, keep growing.

00:20:13.160 --> 00:20:16.973
And that's how you get
the big tumorous mass.

00:20:18.920 --> 00:20:21.384
Did I answer your question?

00:20:21.384 --> 00:20:23.430
- [Mary] Yeah, thank you.

00:20:23.430 --> 00:20:24.263
- Yeah.

00:20:26.610 --> 00:20:27.840
So, let's see.

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:30.310
I think we have a few in the chat.

00:20:30.310 --> 00:20:31.290
So we have somebody who asked

00:20:31.290 --> 00:20:32.980
about it is stem cell research

00:20:32.980 --> 00:20:33.820
still relevant in the labs?

00:20:33.820 --> 00:20:37.083
Yes, stem cell research
is extremely relevant.

00:20:39.810 --> 00:20:41.870
So I'm mostly focused on cancer,

00:20:41.870 --> 00:20:46.020
but I also do some research
looking at development,

00:20:46.020 --> 00:20:48.650
so how red blood cells differentiate.

00:20:48.650 --> 00:20:52.520
And so for me, I use not
quite a full stem cell,

00:20:52.520 --> 00:20:54.790
but like a little bit more along the way.

00:20:54.790 --> 00:20:55.870
And then I induce it

00:20:55.870 --> 00:20:58.760
to differentiate into the red blood cell.

00:20:58.760 --> 00:21:00.590
But a lot of what we're understanding

00:21:00.590 --> 00:21:03.089
about how the body forms

00:21:03.089 --> 00:21:07.140
basically from fertilization
is using stem cells.

00:21:07.140 --> 00:21:11.720
We're also figuring out how
to potentially treat diseases.

00:21:11.720 --> 00:21:15.793
So stem cells are still
very much a relevant field.

00:21:18.560 --> 00:21:19.393
Let's see.

00:21:19.393 --> 00:21:20.660
Have there been experiments

00:21:20.660 --> 00:21:24.030
trying to take the forever
expanding properties of tumors

00:21:24.030 --> 00:21:25.643
and re-purposing them?

00:21:30.150 --> 00:21:33.940
So, Andy, would you mind telling me

00:21:33.940 --> 00:21:36.543
exactly what you were trying to ask here?

00:21:38.270 --> 00:21:39.103
- [Andy] Well, like,

00:21:39.103 --> 00:21:42.320
you see how tumors can
expand forever, right?

00:21:42.320 --> 00:21:44.800
So that would mean,

00:21:44.800 --> 00:21:46.770
well, I'm assuming that that would mean

00:21:46.770 --> 00:21:51.770
that like the tumor mass itself
can live forever, I guess.

00:21:51.850 --> 00:21:52.683
Right?

00:21:52.683 --> 00:21:55.460
So would they take like
some of those properties

00:21:55.460 --> 00:21:58.660
of the tumor itself, and like for example,

00:21:58.660 --> 00:22:00.410
that it can expand forever

00:22:00.410 --> 00:22:02.530
or other properties that it may have,

00:22:02.530 --> 00:22:04.750
and using them for other things,

00:22:04.750 --> 00:22:05.960
or like, I don't know,

00:22:05.960 --> 00:22:09.073
trying to use them for like
medicine or something like that?

00:22:10.237 --> 00:22:11.683
- I mean, I think definitely

00:22:11.683 --> 00:22:14.290
everything we learned
about how cancer works

00:22:14.290 --> 00:22:18.110
helps us understand how things go normally

00:22:18.110 --> 00:22:20.960
'cause you know, if something
is happening abnormally,

00:22:20.960 --> 00:22:23.590
you can suddenly discover
certain signaling pathways

00:22:23.590 --> 00:22:26.500
or interactions between different cells

00:22:26.500 --> 00:22:30.180
that are relevant under
normal developmental

00:22:30.180 --> 00:22:31.410
or normal cases.

00:22:31.410 --> 00:22:35.197
And you can figure out
how they are relevant.

00:22:35.197 --> 00:22:38.240
Same with certain other diseases

00:22:38.240 --> 00:22:40.390
share properties with cancer.

00:22:40.390 --> 00:22:41.860
And so if you can understand

00:22:41.860 --> 00:22:44.610
how this one pathway is
misregulated in cancer,

00:22:44.610 --> 00:22:46.480
you can understand

00:22:46.480 --> 00:22:48.680
how it's misregulated
in this other disease.

00:22:49.710 --> 00:22:53.550
But as far as like taking
their ability to replicate,

00:22:53.550 --> 00:22:58.100
at least I'm not aware
of how that's been used

00:22:58.100 --> 00:23:02.500
other than in the lab where
we have certain cell lines.

00:23:02.500 --> 00:23:06.470
So like, if I took a sample of my blood

00:23:06.470 --> 00:23:08.170
and I tried to grow it in the lab,

00:23:08.170 --> 00:23:09.750
it would die pretty quickly

00:23:09.750 --> 00:23:12.370
because the cells can't grow forever.

00:23:12.370 --> 00:23:14.860
And also, I have to have
the right nutrients.

00:23:14.860 --> 00:23:19.490
If I took the genes that
cancer uses to grow forever

00:23:19.490 --> 00:23:22.210
and I put it into my blood cells

00:23:22.210 --> 00:23:23.140
and tried to grow it in the lab,

00:23:23.140 --> 00:23:24.600
then it would work a lot better.

00:23:24.600 --> 00:23:27.040
And so it's more using it as a tool

00:23:27.040 --> 00:23:29.030
in order to make these cell lines

00:23:29.030 --> 00:23:30.950
that normally would have a limit,

00:23:30.950 --> 00:23:32.570
you're able to grow them forever.

00:23:32.570 --> 00:23:35.163
It's called immortalization
or transformation.

00:23:36.900 --> 00:23:38.850
But I don't know about any cases

00:23:38.850 --> 00:23:41.390
where it's been used beyond that,

00:23:41.390 --> 00:23:43.740
but that doesn't mean that it's not.

00:23:43.740 --> 00:23:45.543
It's just I'm not aware of it.

00:23:47.258 --> 00:23:49.500
It looks like we have one final question.

00:23:49.500 --> 00:23:52.220
So is chemotherapy supposed
to stop the cell growth?

00:23:52.220 --> 00:23:53.520
Yeah.

00:23:53.520 --> 00:23:56.300
So one of the hallmarks of cancer

00:23:56.300 --> 00:23:58.640
is that it grows a lot faster

00:23:58.640 --> 00:24:00.780
than all the other cells in your body.

00:24:00.780 --> 00:24:04.900
And so chemotherapies
basically act upon that.

00:24:04.900 --> 00:24:06.860
They prevent,

00:24:06.860 --> 00:24:10.570
they kill cells that are
rapidly proliferating

00:24:10.570 --> 00:24:13.950
more than they kill cells
that are not growing as fast.

00:24:13.950 --> 00:24:15.820
And so the cancer is more hit,

00:24:15.820 --> 00:24:18.550
but it's the reason why if
you treat with chemotherapy,

00:24:18.550 --> 00:24:19.580
you lose your hair,

00:24:19.580 --> 00:24:21.740
you can have gastrointestinal issues.

00:24:21.740 --> 00:24:25.250
And that's because the cells

00:24:25.250 --> 00:24:27.980
that are producing your
like hair follicles

00:24:27.980 --> 00:24:31.930
or the cells in your gut
also rapidly proliferate.

00:24:31.930 --> 00:24:35.270
And so you're hitting cancer,

00:24:35.270 --> 00:24:36.850
but then you're also
hitting these other cells.

00:24:36.850 --> 00:24:38.770
And then the rest of
the cells in your body

00:24:38.770 --> 00:24:40.700
are kind of spared,

00:24:40.700 --> 00:24:42.580
but that's why you have
these side effects.

00:24:42.580 --> 00:24:46.280
It's very much a blunt
instrument for treating cancer

00:24:46.280 --> 00:24:47.990
although it's done wonders

00:24:47.990 --> 00:24:51.395
and was initially how we
started treating cancer.

00:24:51.395 --> 00:24:54.212
Now, we're starting to be
a little bit more targeted

00:24:54.212 --> 00:24:57.780
by using things that are
specific for the mutations

00:24:57.780 --> 00:24:59.500
in that type of cancer,

00:24:59.500 --> 00:25:03.890
or immunotherapy where you
take the person's immune system

00:25:03.890 --> 00:25:07.780
and basically target the immune
system toward the cancer.

00:25:07.780 --> 00:25:09.720
And yes, the hair does grow back

00:25:09.720 --> 00:25:11.450
because once you remove the chemotherapy,

00:25:11.450 --> 00:25:13.900
the cells are able to come back

00:25:13.900 --> 00:25:15.760
because you have stem cells.

00:25:15.760 --> 00:25:18.310
And potentially, the cancer can come back

00:25:18.310 --> 00:25:20.960
depending on whether you
got rid of all of it or not.

00:25:22.559 --> 00:25:23.829
- [Student] See, I was about to ask that.

00:25:23.829 --> 00:25:25.950
So if one cancer cell remains,

00:25:25.950 --> 00:25:27.200
it does come back, right?

00:25:29.690 --> 00:25:30.523
- Yeah.

00:25:30.523 --> 00:25:32.940
So if you can't get rid of
all of the cancer cells,

00:25:32.940 --> 00:25:34.850
then it can come back.

00:25:34.850 --> 00:25:35.820
And so that's part of the reason

00:25:35.820 --> 00:25:37.880
why we try so many
different ways of treating.

00:25:37.880 --> 00:25:40.260
So sometimes, somebody will get surgery,

00:25:40.260 --> 00:25:42.730
and radiation, and chemotherapy

00:25:42.730 --> 00:25:46.020
because the surgery removes
the bulk of the tumor.

00:25:46.020 --> 00:25:47.730
Then the chemotherapy and the radiation

00:25:47.730 --> 00:25:49.787
can help get those cells
that are remaining.

00:25:49.787 --> 00:25:51.860
And if they get all the
cells that are remaining,

00:25:51.860 --> 00:25:52.800
you're good.

00:25:52.800 --> 00:25:54.490
If they don't,

00:25:54.490 --> 00:25:57.140
which it's sometimes hard
to tell if they do or not,

00:25:57.140 --> 00:26:00.150
then that's when you have
relapse down the line.

00:26:00.150 --> 00:26:03.430
And so one of the major
focuses of cancer research

00:26:03.430 --> 00:26:04.680
is trying to get better and better

00:26:04.680 --> 00:26:07.580
at getting all of those cells,

00:26:07.580 --> 00:26:12.017
targeting every last cell
before you stop the treatment,

00:26:12.017 --> 00:26:13.970
although the treatment's limited.

00:26:13.970 --> 00:26:15.670
You can't train somebody with chemotherapy

00:26:15.670 --> 00:26:19.530
for the rest of their life
because the toxic side effects

00:26:19.530 --> 00:26:23.593
and it possibly could have
other effects down the line.

00:26:24.480 --> 00:26:27.520
It looks like Rosalind has a question.

00:26:27.520 --> 00:26:28.353
- [Rosalind] Yes.

00:26:28.353 --> 00:26:31.240
So I wanted to ask about breast cancer

00:26:31.240 --> 00:26:35.290
'cause I have seen multiple articles

00:26:35.290 --> 00:26:37.290
talk about that people of color,

00:26:37.290 --> 00:26:38.150
well, women of color,

00:26:38.150 --> 00:26:40.597
especially Black women and Hispanic women

00:26:40.597 --> 00:26:44.090
are mostly affected by breast cancer.

00:26:44.090 --> 00:26:45.950
And majority of the time,

00:26:45.950 --> 00:26:48.140
Black women end up actually having more,

00:26:48.140 --> 00:26:50.780
develop more aggressive
cancer and also stage,

00:26:50.780 --> 00:26:52.720
which is why they're
diagnosed at a very young age

00:26:52.720 --> 00:26:55.936
and also end up dying very
quickly compared to white women.

00:26:55.936 --> 00:26:56.769
So I would like to know why maybe.

00:27:00.562 --> 00:27:05.562
- So I'm not an expert in
breast cancer research.

00:27:06.340 --> 00:27:09.630
From my understanding, I
don't think we fully know.

00:27:09.630 --> 00:27:11.700
I think some of it has to do

00:27:11.700 --> 00:27:16.700
with other genes that may be expressed

00:27:17.144 --> 00:27:21.563
in Caucasian versus Black women.

00:27:22.640 --> 00:27:23.473
But, yeah.

00:27:23.473 --> 00:27:26.080
I mean, it's definitely a major issue.

00:27:26.080 --> 00:27:28.823
Cancer is not one disease.

00:27:29.880 --> 00:27:32.080
Basically every single
person who has cancer

00:27:32.080 --> 00:27:34.283
has their own unique disease.

00:27:35.315 --> 00:27:38.570
You know, like the
mutations are different.

00:27:38.570 --> 00:27:41.360
The overall person is different.

00:27:41.360 --> 00:27:42.193
'Cause you know,

00:27:42.193 --> 00:27:46.640
I have certain alleles of genes

00:27:46.640 --> 00:27:48.380
that someone else doesn't

00:27:48.380 --> 00:27:51.170
and we don't entirely know how that plays

00:27:51.170 --> 00:27:52.840
with the mutations in the cancer.

00:27:52.840 --> 00:27:56.320
And so I'm sorry I don't
have a better answer

00:27:56.320 --> 00:27:57.560
other than yes,

00:27:57.560 --> 00:27:59.450
that is something we
are very much aware of

00:27:59.450 --> 00:28:02.287
that there are these differences,

00:28:02.287 --> 00:28:07.023
but we haven't yet fully
figured out what causes them.

00:28:08.530 --> 00:28:09.503
Mary Ellis?

00:28:11.090 --> 00:28:11.923
- [Mary] Okay.

00:28:13.402 --> 00:28:16.200
Because cancer is a mutation to the cells,

00:28:16.200 --> 00:28:19.292
is that why it's able to be passed on?

00:28:19.292 --> 00:28:21.780
That's why if one of your family members

00:28:21.780 --> 00:28:23.500
has like a certain type of cancer,

00:28:23.500 --> 00:28:25.623
it's a higher chance that
you are gonna get it?

00:28:26.730 --> 00:28:28.820
- So, sort of.

00:28:28.820 --> 00:28:32.220
So there are different
contributors to cancer.

00:28:32.220 --> 00:28:33.930
So there's environmental effects,

00:28:33.930 --> 00:28:35.343
for example, smoking.

00:28:36.180 --> 00:28:39.200
There are genetic effects

00:28:39.200 --> 00:28:43.088
and then there's just
kind of random chance.

00:28:43.088 --> 00:28:46.155
And so if I have a mutation,

00:28:46.155 --> 00:28:48.440
for example, skin cancer
arises from moles.

00:28:48.440 --> 00:28:52.529
So I have a mutation that
occurs in this mole on my arm

00:28:52.529 --> 00:28:55.560
that causes me to get melanoma.

00:28:55.560 --> 00:28:57.450
I'm not gonna pass that onto my children

00:28:57.450 --> 00:28:59.480
'cause that mutation arose spontaneously

00:28:59.480 --> 00:29:01.250
in this one skin cell.

00:29:01.250 --> 00:29:04.670
However, if throughout my
entire body, I have a mutation,

00:29:04.670 --> 00:29:09.240
for example, BRCA1, which is
associated with breast cancer,

00:29:09.240 --> 00:29:10.750
and that's throughout my entire body,

00:29:10.750 --> 00:29:13.800
which includes my germ cells, so my eggs.

00:29:13.800 --> 00:29:14.950
When I have a child,

00:29:14.950 --> 00:29:18.730
there's a possibility of
passing that on to my child.

00:29:18.730 --> 00:29:21.810
And depending on the child's father,

00:29:21.810 --> 00:29:24.020
what their genetics are,

00:29:24.020 --> 00:29:28.320
then I can pass on an increased
risk of cancer to my child.

00:29:28.320 --> 00:29:31.550
And so it basically depends
on where the mutation is.

00:29:31.550 --> 00:29:33.920
If the mutation is somatic,

00:29:33.920 --> 00:29:37.510
so it's only in normal body cells,

00:29:37.510 --> 00:29:40.200
so not your egg or sperm cells,

00:29:40.200 --> 00:29:41.880
it won't be passed on.

00:29:41.880 --> 00:29:43.920
If it's throughout the entire body

00:29:43.920 --> 00:29:45.550
or it affects your eggs and your sperm,

00:29:45.550 --> 00:29:48.143
then you can inherit it.

00:29:52.420 --> 00:29:54.610
Yes, and those are good points.

00:29:54.610 --> 00:29:58.150
And then why do auto-immune diseases

00:29:58.150 --> 00:30:00.360
attack the body's healthy cells

00:30:00.360 --> 00:30:01.290
and how does it get treated?

00:30:01.290 --> 00:30:04.530
So I'm not an expert on the immune system,

00:30:04.530 --> 00:30:06.983
but from my understanding,

00:30:06.983 --> 00:30:09.330
so you normally have your T-cells

00:30:09.330 --> 00:30:12.590
and they're trained to
recognize self versus non-self.

00:30:12.590 --> 00:30:13.810
And the ones that recognize self,

00:30:13.810 --> 00:30:16.670
the ones that recognize
like me are weeded out,

00:30:16.670 --> 00:30:17.870
they're killed.

00:30:17.870 --> 00:30:20.690
And it's only the ones
that don't recognize self

00:30:20.690 --> 00:30:25.690
that are able to continue
growing inside of your body.

00:30:27.930 --> 00:30:32.850
And they recognize bacteria,
viruses, cancer cells.

00:30:35.480 --> 00:30:38.370
But in some people,

00:30:38.370 --> 00:30:41.260
the self recognizing
cells, for whatever reason,

00:30:41.260 --> 00:30:43.610
are not killed in that same way.

00:30:43.610 --> 00:30:46.740
And so then they are
able to attack your body

00:30:46.740 --> 00:30:51.459
because they think that it's
a foreign invader basically

00:30:51.459 --> 00:30:54.550
'cause T-cells don't know self for self,

00:30:54.550 --> 00:30:57.440
they just know whatever
they're designed to target.

00:30:57.440 --> 00:30:59.720
And so if whatever
they're designed to target

00:30:59.720 --> 00:31:01.340
happens to be you,

00:31:01.340 --> 00:31:02.870
they're gonna attack you the same way

00:31:02.870 --> 00:31:06.000
that they would attack any other pathogen,

00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:07.503
any other foreign invader.

00:31:10.320 --> 00:31:12.343
