00:32:21.400 --> 00:32:24.670
So, as I said before,
I study colon cancer.

00:32:24.670 --> 00:32:28.940
So it's your large intestine,
cancers that arise there,

00:32:28.940 --> 00:32:32.330
and kind of two major aspects of it.

00:32:32.330 --> 00:32:34.560
The first is trying to study

00:32:34.560 --> 00:32:37.000
the role of red blood cells in cancer.

00:32:37.000 --> 00:32:40.940
And so, if you consider the cancer itself

00:32:40.940 --> 00:32:45.940
as this small community of
cells that are all cancer cells,

00:32:45.950 --> 00:32:47.670
there's also all these normal cells

00:32:47.670 --> 00:32:49.150
that interact with this cancer,

00:32:49.150 --> 00:32:52.770
that surround it, that are all around it.

00:32:52.770 --> 00:32:56.840
And one of the cells
includes red blood cells.

00:32:56.840 --> 00:32:59.170
And so we're interested to see

00:32:59.170 --> 00:33:02.340
how red blood cells can
interact with the cancer cell

00:33:02.340 --> 00:33:04.670
and promote the growth of the cancer.

00:33:04.670 --> 00:33:07.360
So we think that they
might provide nutrients

00:33:07.360 --> 00:33:10.573
and maybe other signals
that promote the growth.

00:33:11.720 --> 00:33:12.553
You can go ahead.

00:33:16.850 --> 00:33:19.170
I mean, I have like five more minutes.

00:33:19.170 --> 00:33:23.454
Okay, sorry.

00:33:23.454 --> 00:33:24.287
So, yeah.

00:33:24.287 --> 00:33:26.120
So that's kind of the first thing

00:33:26.120 --> 00:33:26.953
that I'm trying to study

00:33:26.953 --> 00:33:30.210
is trying to see how the normal cells

00:33:30.210 --> 00:33:31.550
outside of the cancer interacts

00:33:31.550 --> 00:33:32.920
because we're becoming more and more aware

00:33:32.920 --> 00:33:34.510
of the cancer micro environment,

00:33:34.510 --> 00:33:36.780
this environment of normal cells

00:33:36.780 --> 00:33:38.773
actually plays a very
important role in cancer.

00:33:38.773 --> 00:33:41.860
It's not just the cancer
cells in isolation.

00:33:41.860 --> 00:33:43.320
And potentially, we can,

00:33:43.320 --> 00:33:46.200
instead of just trying to
target the cancer cells,

00:33:46.200 --> 00:33:48.540
we can treat and try and
target these normal cells,

00:33:48.540 --> 00:33:49.830
prevent these normal cells

00:33:49.830 --> 00:33:52.900
from giving the cancers
whatever nutrients they need.

00:33:52.900 --> 00:33:56.580
And that's another way we
can possibly try to treat

00:33:56.580 --> 00:33:58.900
and cure a person's cancer.

00:33:58.900 --> 00:34:01.610
And then the other project
that I'm focusing on

00:34:01.610 --> 00:34:05.200
is looking at the role of glucose,

00:34:05.200 --> 00:34:08.023
which is a sugar in colon cancer.

00:34:08.960 --> 00:34:10.860
We know that cancer cells

00:34:10.860 --> 00:34:14.100
actually metabolize glucose much faster

00:34:14.100 --> 00:34:18.330
or much more rapidly
compared to normal cells.

00:34:18.330 --> 00:34:22.730
And this has to do with how
they're metabolically set up.

00:34:22.730 --> 00:34:25.610
And so we're trying to
see how in colon cancer,

00:34:25.610 --> 00:34:27.460
glucose affects the signaling pathway

00:34:27.460 --> 00:34:30.560
called the SAT pathway, a SAT3 pathway,

00:34:30.560 --> 00:34:34.430
and how that promotes
the growth of the tumor.

00:34:34.430 --> 00:34:36.200
And so we can understand

00:34:36.200 --> 00:34:39.270
how glucose leads to
activation of this pathway,

00:34:39.270 --> 00:34:41.510
which promotes the growth of the cancer.

00:34:41.510 --> 00:34:43.480
Then maybe we can, once again,

00:34:43.480 --> 00:34:47.300
come up with new and better
ways to target colon cancer

00:34:47.300 --> 00:34:49.480
'cause colon cancer is one of these ones

00:34:49.480 --> 00:34:50.800
where we can screen for it

00:34:50.800 --> 00:34:54.972
and we can treat it early, which is good.

00:34:54.972 --> 00:34:57.650
And that's part of the reason
why it's highly curable.

00:34:57.650 --> 00:34:58.580
And then there's other people

00:34:58.580 --> 00:35:00.920
that whatever we've tried to this point,

00:35:00.920 --> 00:35:01.870
it just doesn't work.

00:35:01.870 --> 00:35:02.960
And so we're always looking

00:35:02.960 --> 00:35:05.410
for a new understanding of cancer

00:35:05.410 --> 00:35:09.550
so that we can have a new therapy to try.

00:35:09.550 --> 00:35:12.930
And so I'm not involved in
the creation of the therapies,

00:35:12.930 --> 00:35:15.240
but I'm trying to have
a better understanding,

00:35:15.240 --> 00:35:18.360
so that way, other people
can take that understanding

00:35:18.360 --> 00:35:19.983
and convert it into therapies.

00:35:21.175 --> 00:35:24.380
So yeah, that was a very quick overview

00:35:24.380 --> 00:35:26.483
of kind of what I do in lab.

